{
  "result": "I've always liked the moment at airports when your flight has just landed but you haven't yet taken your phone off airplane mode. Everyone around you is suspended. For a few minutes, nobody knows where you are.\n\nNo texts can reach you. No emails. No alerts telling you what thing happened while you were above the clouds. You're in a pocket outside the timeline, and the world has to wait.\n\nWe don't get many pockets anymore. Everything is stitched together - location sharing, read receipts, the little green dot that says you're active. It pretends to be about connection, but a lot of it is about availability. Being constantly findable.\n\nI think that's why people romanticize cabins, and trains without wifi, and notebooks made of paper. Not because they hate technology, but because they miss not being expected to respond.\n\nMy grandfather had a workshop behind his house. Nothing there was efficient. He had jars of nails sorted by size, coffee cans full of screws he would never use, a radio that only picked up one station. He would go out there for hours and do nothing.\n\nI asked him once what he was doing. He said, \"Putting things where they go.\"\n\nI didn't get it then. I get it now. He wasn't organizing the workshop. He was organizing himself.\n\nMaybe freedom isn't about escaping. Maybe it's about having a place to go where nobody needs anything from you for a while, so you can remember what you need from yourself.",
